== Advanced Play: Expert Mechanics

=== Phase 3: Realm Divide & High-Level Strategy

==== How Realm Divide Actually Works

Realm Divide is not a fixed threshold. It is a hidden *fame* score that accumulates from everything you do:

- Owning provinces: +2 fame per province above 20
- Becoming Shogun (holding Kyoto for 4 consecutive turns): +40 fame
- Destroying a major clan (eliminating every member): +15 fame
- Winning heroic victories: +2-5 fame per battle
- Alliances: -2 fame per alliance (alliances delay the Divide, they do not eliminate it)

When your fame score reaches ~75 (on Normal), Realm Divide triggers. On Hard the threshold is ~65. On Very Hard it is ~55.

The turn Realm Divide triggers, every surviving clan that has no treaties with you automatically declares war. Clans that have active treaties (non-aggression, trade, alliance) get a choice — they may join you, stay neutral, or join the coalition against you. Most will join against you.

The only way to prevent Realm Divide is to never reach the fame threshold. This means staying below ~22 provinces (the safe zone) or avoiding heroic victories. Since you must reach 25+ provinces for a Short Campaign victory, Realm Divide is mandatory for completing the game.

==== Pre-Divide Preparation (Turns 10-20 Before)

Start preparing 10-20 turns before you expect Realm Divide. The timing is predictable if you count provinces: divide triggers 1-3 turns after you cross 25 provinces.

*Military preparation:*
- Every border province gets a castle upgrade (minimum tier 3). A tier-3 castle has walls, oil, and a garrison of ~8 units. The AI will waste 3-5 turns besieging each one.
- Every army should be at 18-20 units. Do not have partial stacks when Divide triggers.
- Station one full army at each border region. You need 1 army per 3-4 border provinces.
- Your main army (the best general, the best units) stays in a central position where it can reinforce any border within 2 turns.

*Economic preparation:*
- Stockpile 20,000-30,000 koku. You will not be able to trade after Divide triggers.
- All provinces that can build a market should build a market. Your peacetime economy must support wartime spending.
- Build roads on all border provinces. Faster army movement means you can defend twice as many provinces with the same number of armies.

*Diplomatic preparation:*
- Break all alliances 5-10 turns before you expect Divide. Each broken alliance costs -10 honor, but a clan that is neutral toward you has a small chance of staying neutral during Divide. A clan that is allied when Divide triggers gets a massive diplomacy penalty — they almost always join the coalition.
- Leave 1-2 non-aggression pacts active. These give a small chance the clan stays neutral.
- Give small gifts (1,000 koku) to your most powerful neighbors. This improves their relationship score, making them slightly more likely to stay neutral.

==== Post-Divide Strategy: The First 10 Turns

The first 10 turns after Realm Divide determine whether you survive. The AI has a massive military advantage at this point — every clan in Japan has full armies and they all target you.

*Turn 1-3 (the initial wave):*
- Do not advance anywhere. Pull back all armies to defensive positions.
- Every army should be inside a castle or on a river crossing with a hill behind it.
- Use your navy to blockade the 2-3 most active enemy ports. A blockaded port cannot produce new units.
- Spend your stockpiled koku: recruit emergency garrisons in provinces without castles. A single yari ashigaru unit adds +3 happiness and +1 garrison.

*Turn 4-7 (the first cracks):*
- The AI will attack your best-defended positions first. Let them. Fight every siege defense manually.
- Siege defense is the most lopsided battle in Shogun 2. A garrison of 8 yari ashigaru can defeat 2 full stacks if you fight manually. The defender gets: wall combat bonus, unlimited ammunition for bow units, oil at the gates (kills 100+ models per pour), and reduced fatigue from not moving.
- Pick the weakest enemy army and destroy it with your central reserve. A full stack elimination (-20 units) reduces the total enemy strength significantly. Most AI clans will struggle to replace 20 units quickly.

*Turn 8-10 (stabilization):*
- The initial AI rush will slow as their armies are depleted.
- Now you counter-attack. Take one strategic province (a trade node, a choke point, or an enemy capital).
- Do not advance beyond one province. Consolidate, repair walls, recruit.

==== The Siege Timing Formula

Siege offense is the hardest skill in Shogun 2. Here is the exact timing:

1. Arrive at the enemy castle. Build 2 siege towers. This takes 2 turns.
2. Attack on the turn the towers complete. The AI repairs broken walls between turns — do not give them that turn.
3. Deploy both towers on the same wall section. Concentrating your force beats spreading it out.
4. Send 2 units up each tower. While they fight on the walls, send a ram unit to the gate.
5. Once the gate breaks (2-3 turns of ramming), send your cavalry and general through.
6. Capture the center. The battle ends when you hold the center for 60 seconds — you do not need to kill every defender.

Starvation (besieging without attacking): a castle with a full garrison holds 4-6 turns of food on Normal difficulty. After food runs out, the garrison takes 20% casualties per turn. On turn 4 of starvation, the garrison is at 50% strength. Starving is safer than assaulting but gives the enemy time to reinforce. Only starve tier-4 castles (Citadel+) where assaulting would cost too many lives.

==== Navy Dominance

Naval power is the most overlooked mechanic by intermediate players. Here is how it works:

*Ship types:*
- *Bow Kobaya:* Fast, cheap, 15 crew. Use for scouting and trade node occupation.
- *Bow Medium:* 40 crew, 10 bowmen. Your standard fighting ship. Can blockade ports.
- *Wako (Rise of the Samurai):* Heaviest normal ship. Very effective in boarding actions.
- *Black Ship:* Unique Portuguese carrack. 60 crew, 20 cannon (refire). One-shots any other ship. Captured via boarding — reduce crew to ~30 via cannon fire, then board. Once yours, it wins every naval battle automatically.

*Blockade mechanics:*
A blockaded port cannot recruit ships. A blockaded port generates 50% less tax income. Blockading a clan's only port cuts their naval capacity to zero. Blockade is not an act of war in itself — but the targeted clan will dislike you.

*Transport interdiction:*
Enemy armies at sea on transport ships are the most vulnerable target in the game. A single bow medium ship can sink a transport carrying 20 units. The army is destroyed (only the general survives in most cases). This is the most efficient way to reduce enemy strength — one ship costing 300 koku destroys 20 units worth 5,000+ koku.

*Trade node strategy:*
Never leave a trade node undefended. The AI will try to capture it. Park 2 bow medium ships at each node you control. When an enemy approaches, attack before they reach the node.

==== Agent Synergies at Scale

Deep into a campaign, you should have 4-5 agents of each type. Coordinate them:

*Province disruption chain:*
1. Monk: Incite Revolt in target province (reduces happiness by -10 over 3 turns)
2. Ninja: Sabotage Castle Walls (reduces wall HP by 50%)
3. Ninja: Assassinate General (if an army is in the province)
4. Army: Attack with full stack
5. Metsuke: Capture Town (prevents revolt for 3 turns after conquest)

This chain lets one army conquer a tier-4 province with 80% fewer casualties than a direct assault.

*The three-agent scout:*
1. Ninja (Way of the Wind, max movement skills) moves ahead of the army
2. When the ninja spots an enemy stack, a second ninja (assassination spec) targets the general
3. If the assassination fails, the metsuke (crackdown spec) moves in to arrest any enemy counter-agents

=== Phase 4: Mastery & Mod Integration

==== Advanced Province Specialization with Math

The difference between an average player and an expert player is province specialization.

*Compound growth calculation:*
A province developed starting at turn 10 versus turn 50:
- Turn 10 start: 40 turns of compound growth × 3% per turn = 3.3× baseline wealth
- Turn 50 start: 0 turns of compound growth (war starts soon after) = 1× baseline wealth

This is why developing economy early, even when it feels like you should be recruiting armies, is always correct. One farm built at turn 5 pays for itself by turn 15 and generates pure profit from turn 15-80.

*Ideal province grid:*
- *Kyoto:* Economic capital. All market slots, metsuke stationed permanently. Towers generate +50% trade income.
- *Your home province:* Military capital. Max castle, all recruitment buildings, armor smith, horse dojo. Produces units in 1 turn.
- *Coastal provinces:* Port + market in every coastal slot. Trade nodes, navy recruitment.
- *Inland farms:* 3-4 farms + road. Nothing else. These are food-only provinces that feed your military provinces.
- *Border provinces:* Castle + road + 1 farm. These are forts — their economic output is secondary to their defensive value.

==== The Four Army Doctrine

In the late game, organize your armies into four distinct types:

*Sweeper army:* 14 yari ashigaru, 4 bow ashigaru, 1 general, 1 metsuke. Purpose: capture undefended provinces and serve as emergency garrison. Each unit costs 200-300 koku. A full stack costs ~5,000 koku and can be recruited in 2 turns. Use 2-3 sweeper armies to occupy territory faster than the enemy can react.

*Strike army:* 4 yari, 6 katana, 4 naginata, 2 matchlock, 2 bow, 2 cavalry. Purpose: destroy enemy main forces. This army costs ~12,000 koku and takes 5+ turns to recruit. You want 1-2 of these.

*Siege army:* 6 yari, 2 naginata, 4 matchlock, 6 bow, 2 cavalry. Purpose: capture high-tier castles. Extra ranged units for wall suppression. General has the *Engineer* retinue (faster siege construction).

*Naval army:* 6 bow medium ships, 2 Wako/Black Ship. Purpose: control the seas, blockade ports, interdict transports. A naval army costs ~3,000 koku and takes 3 turns to recruit.

==== Opening Moves for Hard Clan Campaigns

After completing a campaign as a strong clan (Shimazu, Oda, Tokugawa), try these:

*Imagawa (Very Hard start):*
- You are sandwiched between Oda to the east and Tokugawa to the west.
- Turn 1: Send a gift of 1,000 koku to Oda. Build a farm. Recruit yari ashigaru.
- Turn 2: Move your army toward Tokugawa. Attack them before they attack you.
- Turns 3-20: Fight a defensive war against Oda while conquering Tokugawa territory. Do not let Oda catch you in the open — fight from castles.
- Key advantage: Imagawa gets +2 honor from trade. Use trade as your economy engine.

*Mori (naval focus):*
- You control the western trade nodes. Build trade ships on turn 1 and send them to the nearest node.
- Your economy comes from the sea, not the land. You can afford fewer armies but better ones.
- Key advantage: Mori bow ships are the best in the game. Dominate the seas, blockade your enemies, and starve their economies.

*Hojo (development focus):*
- You start in the Kanto region (eastern Japan) with the highest development potential in the game.
- Build farms and markets in every province for the first 20 turns. Do not recruit more than one army.
- Key advantage: Hojo get +1 to castle tiers. Your castles are always one level above everyone else's. A Hojo tier-3 castle is effectively a tier-4 castle.

==== Realm Divide: The Minimal Province Strategy

The most advanced Realm Divide strategy is to deliberately delay it:

1. Stay below 22 provinces. Never cross the threshold.
2. Raze (sack + abandon) provinces beyond 22 instead of occupying them.
3. Build 3-4 fully-developed core provinces with max-tier farms, markets, and castles.
4. Build 4 full strike armies.
5. In one turn: occupy 5-10 razed provinces (they cost nothing militarily — no garrisons).
6. Realm Divide triggers. You now have 30+ provinces and 4 full armies.
7. The initial AI wave hits your well-fortified core provinces and breaks against them.
8. Counter-attack with your strike armies while the AI is recovering from their failed offensive.

This strategy requires discipline. Every time you see an undefended province and want to take it, ask: *Is this province worth triggering Realm Divide over?* If the answer is no, raze it.

=== Moving Beyond

After completing this curriculum, you have mastered Total War: SHOGUN 2. The game opens into new challenges:

- *Multiplayer:* The competitive scene on Steam uses house rules (no matchlock spam, no cavalry doomstacks). Join the Shogun 2 discord for organized matches.
- *Modding:* Use the Assembly Kit (free on Steam) to create custom units, factions, and campaign maps. The TWC modding forums have tutorials.
- *Legendary difficulty:* No manual saves, no pausing, the AI gets maximum bonuses. Complete a Legendary campaign as every clan.

